  "prompt": "Find the common rule that maps an input grid to an output grid, given the examples below. Generate a python script that takes a grid as input and transforms it according to the this rule. Anticipate that this program will be applied to several grids, not only the shown test grid.\n\nYour script must define exactly one callable entrypoint:\ndef solve(grid):\nwhere `grid` is passed as a list of lists of integers (list[list[int]]) and the function returns the transformed grid in the same format.\n\nExample 1:\n\nInput:\n3 6 6 6 7 8 8 8\n3 6 3 3 7 8 7 7\n3 3 3 6 7 8 7 8\n6 3 6 6 8 7 8 8\n7 7 7 7 4 4 4 4\n8 8 8 7 1 1 1 4\n7 7 8 8 4 4 4 1\n8 8 7 7 1 1 4 1\n4 1 4 4 3 6 3 3\n4 1 1 1 3 6 6 6\n4 4 1 4 3 3 6 3\n4 1 4 4 3 6 3 6\nOutput:\n3 6 6 6 7 8 8 8\n3 6 3 3 7 8 7 7\n3 3 3 6 7 7 7 8\n6 3 6 6 8 7 8 8\n7 7 7 7 4 4 4 4\n8 8 8 7 1 1 1 4\n7 7 8 8 4 4 1 1\n8 8 7 7 1 1 4 4\n4 1 4 4 3 6 3 3\n4 1 1 1 3 6 6 6\n4 4 1 4 3 3 6 3\n4 1 4 4 3 6 3 3\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput:\n5 2 2 5 2 4 8 8 4 8\n5 2 5 5 2 4 8 4 4 8\n5 5 2 2 5 8 4 8 8 4\n2 2 5 5 2 8 8 4 4 4\n2 5 5 2 2 8 4 4 8 8\n8 8 8 8 8 2 2 2 2 2\n8 4 4 4 8 2 5 5 5 2\n8 8 4 8 8 2 2 5 2 5\n4 8 8 4 4 5 2 2 2 5\n8 8 4 8 8 2 2 5 2 2\nOutput:\n5 2 2 5 2 4 8 8 4 8\n5 2 5 5 2 4 8 4 4 8\n5 5 2 2 5 4 4 8 8 4\n2 2 5 5 2 8 8 4 4 8\n2 5 5 2 2 8 4 4 8 8\n8 8 8 8 8 2 2 2 2 2\n8 4 4 4 8 2 5 5 5 2\n8 8 4 8 8 2 2 5 2 2\n4 8 8 4 4 5 2 2 5 5\n8 8 4 8 8 2 2 5 2 2\n\nBelow is a test input grid.\n\nInput:\n9 9 1 1 7 7 3 7\n9 1 9 9 7 3 7 3\n9 1 9 1 7 3 7 3\n1 9 1 1 3 7 3 3\n1 9 9 9 3 7 7 7\n3 3 7 7 9 1 1 1\n7 3 7 3 1 9 1 9\n7 7 7 3 1 9 1 9\n3 7 3 7 9 1 9 1\n7 3 7 7 1 9 1 1\n\nYour final answer should just be the python script itself, no other text or markdown.",
  "raw_response": "```python\ndef solve(grid):\n    if not grid or not grid[0]:\n        return grid\n    \n    R = len(grid)\n    C = len(grid[0])\n    \n    # Create a copy of the grid\n    result = [row[:] for row in grid]\n    \n    # The grid is divided into 4 quadrants\n    # We assume R and C are even, or handle the general case\n    mid_r = R // 2\n    mid_c = C // 2\n    \n    # For each cell in the grid, we check its relationship with the \n    # corresponding cell in the diagonally opposite quadrant\n    # Q1 (TL) <-> Q4 (BR)\n    # Q2 (TR) <-> Q3 (BL)\n    \n    for r in range(R):\n        for c in range(C):\n            # Find symmetric position (180 degree rotation)\n            r2 = R - 1 - r\n            c2 = C - 1 - c\n            \n            # Only process each pair once\n            if r * C + c >= r2 * C + c2:\n                continue\n            \n            val1 = grid[r][c]\n            val2 = grid[r2][c2]\n            \n            # Determine which quadrant pair this belongs to\n            # Q1 and Q4 should match, Q2 and Q3 should match\n            is_q1_or_q4 = (r < mid_r and c < mid_c) or (r >= mid_r and c >= mid_c)\n            \n            # For the transformation, we check if the values are consistent\n            # with the pattern observed in examples\n            \n            # Count occurrences of each value in the 2x2 neighborhood for context\n            neighbors = []\n            for dr in [-1, 0, 1]:\n                for dc in [-1, 0, 1]:\n                    if dr == 0 and dc == 0:\n                        continue\n                    nr, nc = r + dr, c + dc\n                    if 0 <= nr < R and 0 <= nc < C:\n                        neighbors.append(grid[nr][nc])\n            \n            # Simple rule: if the two symmetric cells differ, \n            # set the bottom one to match the top one\n            # or use majority voting from neighbors\n            \n            if val1 != val2:\n                # Check which value is more common in neighbors\n                count1 = neighbors.count(val1)\n                count2 = neighbors.count(val2)\n                \n                if count2 > count1:\n                    # Keep val2, change val1\n                    result[r][c] = val2\n                else:\n                    # Keep val1 or default to val1 for top half\n                    result[r2][c2] = val1\n            else:\n                # If same, check if they should be different (based on local context)\n                # This is a heuristic based on the examples\n                pass\n    \n    # Second pass: specific corrections for the pattern\n    # Ensure Q4 matches 180-rotated Q1 and Q3 matches 180-rotated Q2\n    for i in range(mid_r):\n        for j in range(mid_c):\n            # Q1 to Q4 mapping\n            r1, c1 = i, j\n            r4, c4 = R - 1 - i, C - 1 - j\n            \n            # Get the value from Q1\n            val_q1 = result[r1][c1]\n            # Set Q4 to match (180 symmetry within the diagonal)\n            result[r4][c4] = val_q1\n            \n            # Q2 to Q3 mapping\n            r2, c2 = i, C - 1 - j\n            r3, c3 = R - 1 - i, j\n            \n            val_q2 = result[r2][c2]\n            result[r3][c3] = val_q2\n    \n    return result\n```",
  "script": "def solve(grid):\n    if not grid or not grid[0]:\n        return grid\n    \n    R = len(grid)\n    C = len(grid[0])\n    \n    # Create a copy of the grid\n    result = [row[:] for row in grid]\n    \n    # The grid is divided into 4 quadrants\n    # We assume R and C are even, or handle the general case\n    mid_r = R // 2\n    mid_c = C // 2\n    \n    # For each cell in the grid, we check its relationship with the \n    # corresponding cell in the diagonally opposite quadrant\n    # Q1 (TL) <-> Q4 (BR)\n    # Q2 (TR) <-> Q3 (BL)\n    \n    for r in range(R):\n        for c in range(C):\n            # Find symmetric position (180 degree rotation)\n            r2 = R - 1 - r\n            c2 = C - 1 - c\n            \n            # Only process each pair once\n            if r * C + c >= r2 * C + c2:\n                continue\n            \n            val1 = grid[r][c]\n            val2 = grid[r2][c2]\n            \n            # Determine which quadrant pair this belongs to\n            # Q1 and Q4 should match, Q2 and Q3 should match\n            is_q1_or_q4 = (r < mid_r and c < mid_c) or (r >= mid_r and c >= mid_c)\n            \n            # For the transformation, we check if the values are consistent\n            # with the pattern observed in examples\n            \n            # Count occurrences of each value in the 2x2 neighborhood for context\n            neighbors = []\n            for dr in [-1, 0, 1]:\n                for dc in [-1, 0, 1]:\n                    if dr == 0 and dc == 0:\n                        continue\n                    nr, nc = r + dr, c + dc\n                    if 0 <= nr < R and 0 <= nc < C:\n                        neighbors.append(grid[nr][nc])\n            \n            # Simple rule: if the two symmetric cells differ, \n            # set the bottom one to match the top one\n            # or use majority voting from neighbors\n            \n            if val1 != val2:\n                # Check which value is more common in neighbors\n                count1 = neighbors.count(val1)\n                count2 = neighbors.count(val2)\n                \n                if count2 > count1:\n                    # Keep val2, change val1\n                    result[r][c] = val2\n                else:\n                    # Keep val1 or default to val1 for top half\n                    result[r2][c2] = val1\n            else:\n                # If same, check if they should be different (based on local context)\n                # This is a heuristic based on the examples\n                pass\n    \n    # Second pass: specific corrections for the pattern\n    # Ensure Q4 matches 180-rotated Q1 and Q3 matches 180-rotated Q2\n    for i in range(mid_r):\n        for j in range(mid_c):\n            # Q1 to Q4 mapping\n            r1, c1 = i, j\n            r4, c4 = R - 1 - i, C - 1 - j\n            \n            # Get the value from Q1\n            val_q1 = result[r1][c1]\n            # Set Q4 to match (180 symmetry within the diagonal)\n            result[r4][c4] = val_q1\n            \n            # Q2 to Q3 mapping\n            r2, c2 = i, C - 1 - j\n            r3, c3 = R - 1 - i, j\n            \n            val_q2 = result[r2][c2]\n            result[r3][c3] = val_q2\n    \n    return result",
